WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
(gentle music)

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Narrator] Researchers around the world

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.023
are hustling to create this test kit.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.070
The rapidly-spreading new
coronavirus has put test kits

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.960
at the center of how infected
patients are diagnosed,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.660
so they can get treatment quickly.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.450
But there's one issue with these

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.550
nose and throat-swabbing tests.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.443
The results aren't always accurate.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.760
At the center of the epidemic in Wuhan,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.570
some people tested negative,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.800
only later to find out that
they actually have the disease.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.300
In the U.S., the CDC sent out test kits

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.520
to public health labs
that gave inconclusive

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.950
results in the verification process.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.250
The unreliability of
these tests even forced

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.970
Chinese health officials to expand

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.020
how they classify patients,
which resulted in a surge

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.020
of more than 14,000 cases in one day.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.940
They are now including
results from test kits

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.880
and other diagnostic
methods like chest scans.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.890
So how do these test kits exactly work,

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.903
and why are there so many problems?

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.080
This government lab in
Singapore has produced

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.550
test kits for the
country's public hospitals.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.660
It's also shipped about
10,000 tests to China.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.630
Dr. Sidney Yee leads the
team that developed them.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.860
She says most doctors
use a type of lab test

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.140
called RT PCR, which can
be used to detect small

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.310
amounts of pathogens
including viruses like HIV.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.580
- So this is the gold
standard that's being used.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.000
What it does is, it
actually directly detects

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.370
the presence of the pathogen.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.100
- [Narrator] It's pretty easy to use.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.940
A doctor collects samples from a patient

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.700
by swabbing the nose or throat for mucus.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.860
Those swabs are then
sent to a lab for a test

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.180
that detects the genetic
material of a pathogen.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.440
The sample is first mixed
with reagents in a tube,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.370
then put into a machine that duplicates

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.870
the genetic material.
(machine whirring)

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.380
So if the virus exists,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.680
these copies will amplify its presence,

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.580
confirming that a patient has tested

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.100
positive for the coronavirus.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.940
Dr. Yee says the first place for error

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.770
could be at the swabbing stage.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.840
- Most of the time, the
sampling for COVID-19

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.550
comes from a throat swab.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.910
COVID-19 is actually a
lung infection disease,

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:11.230
so doing the throat swab
really depends on how much

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.910
of the pathogen you're able to capture.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.400
- [Narrator] Another place
for error is the time

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.540
it takes for the sample to reach the lab.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.940
- [Sidney] The pathogen on
the swab is not going to last

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.190
for many, many hours,
so that transportation,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.570
the logistics, is also important.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.200
- [Narrator] Transportation
is a huge hurdle in Wuhan,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.420
where the city has been on
lockdown since mid-January.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.810
- [Sidney] When the
sample gets to the lab,

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.960
what is the infrastructure of the lab

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.650
that is able to deal with running tests,

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.670
as well as the expertise
and the experience

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.513
of the lab technicians that
are running these tests?

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.140
- [Narrator] Dr. Yee says
the most critical moment

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.100
for human error is when doctors
decide to use the test kit.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.440
For instance, many people have
been showing mild symptoms,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.130
but that doesn't mean they
don't have the pathogen.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.300
- It's very hard to make
the direct correlation

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.100
between what our test kit can be used,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.530
on which stage of the disease progression,

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.270
with respect to how much the pathogen

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.340
is present in the patients.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.990
- [Narrator] Just how
accurately these test kits

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.820
are being used is on trial in Wuhan.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.870
Here, hospitals have been overstretched

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:18.820
with limited resources.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.000
There are not enough
staff to swab patients,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.890
and labs are inundated with the backlog

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.650
of samples that wait to be tested.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.180
- [Sidney] There are many,
many different steps,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.490
and different processes
involved in just running a test.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:35.169
It's not just with respect
to the test kit itself.

00:03:35.169 --> 00:03:37.219
(gentle music)

